Stop the DRC frontend dropping END_SEQUENCE with a reclaimed skip-slot desc.

THE CRASH THIS FIXES. gaim 0.64 signing in to the retronet OSCAR gateway
killed MAME with SIGSEGV 20-40 s after the session established, 3/3,
while 83 MB of HTTP through the same emulated SEEQ never faulted once
(docs/lab/retronet/ICQ-STATION-irix.md). Wrapper + strace + core on a rig
clone: SEGV_MAPERR at NULL in mips3_device::code_compile_block -- the
"find the last instruction in this sequence" walk found no desc carrying
END_SEQUENCE, walked off the NULL end of the live list, and the
assert(seqlast != nullptr) above the deref is compiled out in release.

THE DEFECT (upstream, src/devices/cpu/drcfe.ipp build_sequence). When a
branch-LIKELY sits at the last word of a mapped page and its delay slot
lands on a page that is not TLB-resident at describe time, the delay-slot
instruction's array desc is both (a) the segment's last instruction -- so
build_sequence gives IT the END_SEQUENCE marker -- and (b) in the branch's
skip slot -- so the skip-slot logic reclaims it instead of appending it,
and the end marker is dropped with it. Read out of the diagnosis core:
BGEZALL (0x0413ff68) at guest pc 0x5fecbffc, delay desc at 0x5fecc000
with COMPILER_PAGE_FAULT|END_SEQUENCE, live-list tail = the branch, no
END anywhere in its sequence. Needs all three at once -- branch-likely at
page end, next page non-resident, block compiled cold -- which is why only
gaim's never-before-executed OSCAR/conversation code ever lined it up.

THE FIX, two halves:
  1. build_sequence: before reclaiming a skipped desc, propagate its
     end_sequence()/redispatch()/return_to_start() to the last APPENDED
     desc (the branch itself). The live list can then never end
     unterminated. This is the root fix and is generic drcfe -- any DRC
     CPU with skip slots (MIPS3 branch-likely) is exposed, not just sgi.
  2. mips3drc code_compile_block: if the END walk still comes up empty,
     recover to the sequence's tail desc and say so via
     osd_printf_error, instead of dereferencing null. Defence in depth:
     it turns any OTHER latent frontend path of the same shape from a
     fleet-visible crash into one log line.

Same change, same bytes, lands as commit 25c6b0bb2b5 on the mame-irix
submodule's `irix` branch; keep the two in parity (see
irix-mame-stack.sh's header for why a drifted copy is worse than none).

diff --git a/src/devices/cpu/drcfe.ipp b/src/devices/cpu/drcfe.ipp
index ce6cf04efba..d9063e115e9 100644
--- a/src/devices/cpu/drcfe.ipp
+++ b/src/devices/cpu/drcfe.ipp
@@ -325,7 +325,29 @@ void drc_frontend_base<Desc>::build_sequence(int start, int end, bool redispatch
 			if (skipsleft == 0)
 				m_desc_live_list.append(*curdesc);
 			else
+			{
+				// a skipped desc can be the one carrying the sequence's end marker: a
+				// branch-likely at the last word of a page whose delay slot sits on a
+				// non-resident page makes its (page-faulting) skip-slot desc the
+				// segment's last instruction, and reclaiming it here would drop
+				// END_SEQUENCE with it, leaving the live list's final sequence
+				// unterminated - the recompilers' END_SEQUENCE walks then run off the
+				// NULL end of the list.  Hand the marker to the last appended desc
+				// (the branch itself) before dropping the skipped one.
+				if (curdesc->end_sequence())
+				{
+					Desc *const lastdesc = m_desc_live_list.last();
+					if (lastdesc != nullptr)
+					{
+						lastdesc->set_end_sequence();
+						if (curdesc->redispatch())
+							lastdesc->set_redispatch();
+						if (curdesc->return_to_start())
+							lastdesc->set_return_to_start();
+					}
+				}
 				m_desc_allocator.reclaim(*curdesc);
+			}
 
 			// if the current instruction starts skipping, reset our skip count
 			// otherwise, just decrement
diff --git a/src/devices/cpu/mips/mips3drc.cpp b/src/devices/cpu/mips/mips3drc.cpp
index ca0bc82199d..eff242e0ea4 100644
--- a/src/devices/cpu/mips/mips3drc.cpp
+++ b/src/devices/cpu/mips/mips3drc.cpp
@@ -330,9 +330,22 @@ void mips3_device::code_compile_block(uint8_t mode, offs_t pc)
 					block.append_comment("-------------------------");                     // comment
 
 				/* determine the last instruction in this sequence */
+				const opcode_desc *seqtail = seqhead;
 				for (seqlast = seqhead; seqlast != nullptr; seqlast = seqlast->next())
+				{
+					seqtail = seqlast;
 					if (seqlast->end_sequence())
 						break;
+				}
+				if (seqlast == nullptr)
+				{
+					/* an unterminated tail should no longer be possible (drcfe.ipp's
+					   build_sequence now propagates END_SEQUENCE past the skip-slot
+					   reclaim), but a null here was a hard crash in release builds:
+					   recover by treating the final description as the sequence end */
+					osd_printf_error("code_compile_block: sequence at %08X has no END_SEQUENCE; using tail %08X\n", seqhead->pc, seqtail->pc);
+					seqlast = seqtail;
+				}
 				assert(seqlast != nullptr);
 
 				/* if we don't have a hash for this mode/pc, or if we are overriding all, add one */
